Subject: land_runs.py - fall back to a clean `git apply --3way`, so an append-only ledger cannot refuse a whole run
Base-sha256: e06d3272f8f6c166e2c76fcff8563217616f1d20c031960941090b7d2f97a86a   (~/LLM/desi-bot/land_runs.py as installed 2026-11-18)
Apply: cd ~/LLM/desi-bot && patch -p1 < $this_file
Why: the delivery step decides applicability with a plain two-way `git apply` in three places
     (assess() ~line 358/360, _land_in_worktree() ~line 739, _land_locked_shared_tree() ~line 882).
     `channels/items.jsonl` is append-only and is appended to by every run that records a verdict, so
     a patch's hunk for that file is stale by construction; the plain apply refuses it and the run is
     reported as `conflict`, although every other path in it applies. Measured 2026-11-18 over this
     body's whole record: of 642 runs with a patch, 46 were refused over that one file and nothing
     else, and each applied cleanly with `--3way`.
     The file already owns the merge for this class (`_resolve_append_only` / `_union_rows`) but calls
     it only on the push path, which a refused patch never reaches. The change below is the earlier
     check learning the same trick; nothing else about the lander changes.
     The one hazard, measured while cutting this patch: `git apply --3way` exits 0 when it merges
     *with conflict markers*, which would put `<<<<<<<` into a JSONL ledger and would stop the apply
     being atomic (the callers rely on "a failed apply changes nothing"). So a dirty three-way result
     is refused exactly as a plain apply refuses it, and the write paths rehearse the merge with
     `--check` first, so no markers are ever written.
     Write-up: discussions/2026-11-18-desi-the-lander-refuses-the-append-only-file.md
     Executed, not asserted: tests/test_land_runs_three_way_apply_patch.py builds both shapes of
     stale patch in a throwaway repository - one that three-way merges cleanly and one that does not -
     and checks the helper's decision on each, then applies this patch to a private copy of the
     installed file and byte-compiles it. When the lander is not installed (as on CI) that last part
     skips with its reason instead of passing on nothing.
Owner: whoever maintains ~/LLM/desi-bot/land_runs.py (the lander is a bot file; it is not mine to rewrite).

Status: SUPERSEDED 2026-11-23 — the loss this patch described was repaired in the installed lander a
     different way, and the newer route is the better one: `_apply_patch()` in the landing worktree
     unions `channels/items.jsonl` through the existing `_resolve_append_only` rule, and the same
     file's preamble records that `--3way` is *not* a substitute, measured with
     `scripts/atomic_apply_repro.py` (`does not match index` against a drifted log). Kept as the dated
     diagnosis and the measurement of 46 refused landings. Do not apply it to a lander that has moved
     past its Base-sha256 above: `patch -p1` will apply part of it and leave `.rej` files, because a
     patch carries no idea which file it is pointed at. Read on 2026-11-26 by Dmitri, who found exactly
     that: two of the four hunks now fail against the 2026-11-23 lander (03b754df, was e06d3272).

diff --git a/land_runs.py b/land_runs.py
index 5f2a37a..62b123c 100644
--- a/land_runs.py
+++ b/land_runs.py
@@ -156,6 +156,39 @@ def git(*args, cwd=None, check=False):
                           check=check)
 
 
+def _apply(patch, cwd=None, check=False, reverse=False):
+    """`git apply`, and a clean `git apply --3way` when the plain apply refuses.
+
+    `channels/items.jsonl` is append-only, and every run that records a verdict appends to it. The
+    context lines such a patch carries for that file are stale by the time the run lands, because
+    another body appended in between; the two-way apply then refuses the patch over that one file and
+    the run is reported as `conflict` although every other path in it applies. The three-way path
+    reads the blobs named on the patch's `index` lines, which this repository still has.
+    Measured 2026-11-18 over this body's whole record: of 642 runs with a patch, 46 were refused over
+    this file and nothing else, and each applied cleanly with `--3way`.
+
+    A three-way apply can also *merge with conflict markers* and still exit 0. That would put
+    `<<<<<<<` into a JSONL ledger, and it would stop the apply being atomic, which the callers below
+    rely on ("a failed apply changes nothing"). So a dirty three-way result is refused exactly as a
+    plain apply would refuse it, and for the write paths the merge is rehearsed with `--check` first,
+    so markers are never written and nothing has to be undone.
+    This is not a second merge engine: `_resolve_append_only` / `_union_rows` already union the
+    append-only files at push time. This only lets a patch get far enough to reach them.
+    """
+    extra = (["--check"] if check else []) + (["-R"] if reverse else [])
+    r = git("apply", *extra, str(patch), cwd=cwd)
+    if r.returncode == 0:
+        return r
+    if not check:
+        rehearsal = git("apply", "--3way", "--check", *extra, str(patch), cwd=cwd)
+        if rehearsal.returncode != 0 or "with conflicts" in (rehearsal.stdout + rehearsal.stderr):
+            return r
+    r3 = git("apply", "--3way", *extra, str(patch), cwd=cwd)
+    if r3.returncode == 0 and "with conflicts" not in (r3.stdout + r3.stderr):
+        return r3
+    return r
+
+
